+# Stock reservations — specification
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout needs to set stock aside for a cart **before** payment, then either
+turn that hold into an order (payment succeeded) or give the stock back
+(payment failed / cart abandoned). Carts that are simply abandoned must not
+lock stock forever, so holds lapse on their own. The cart service retries on
+network failures, so creating a hold must be safe to repeat (idempotency key).
+
+## Interface (fixed — hidden tests call exactly this)
+
+`InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`,
+  `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU),
+  `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`;
+  enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`,
+  `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+  `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass
+  `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry
+  must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+## Definitions
+
+- **Held** (per SKU, per warehouse): the sum of `items[sku]` over reservations
+  in that warehouse whose status is `ACTIVE`.
+- **Available** (per SKU, per warehouse):
+  `max(0, on_hand − allocated − held)`, where *allocated* is units on `PLACED`
+  orders (unchanged meaning).
+- **Hold lifetime**: 20 minutes by default.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Constructor and configuration
+
+1. `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` with exactly those three
+   arguments still works, and holds created through it last 20 minutes.
+2. The hold lifetime can be set with an optional keyword argument to
+   `InventoryService`. When the argument is omitted, the lifetime is 20 minutes.
+3. All existing tests in `tests/` still pass unchanged.
+
+### Creating a hold: `reserve`
+
+4. A successful `reserve` returns a `Reservation` with status `ACTIVE`,
+   `order_id is None`, `warehouse` equal to the requested warehouse, and
+   `expires_at == clock.now() at creation + hold lifetime`.
+5. Each requested line holds `min(requested, available right now)` units for
+   that SKU in that warehouse.
+6. A line with 0 units available does not appear in `items` at all. No key
+   in `items` has the value 0.
+7. If every requested line has 0 units available, `reserve` raises
+   `InsufficientStock` and creates nothing: no reservation exists afterwards,
+   availability is unchanged, and the idempotency key stays unused.
+8. Input is validated like `place_order`:
+   - an unknown warehouse raises `UnknownWarehouse`;
+   - an unknown SKU raises `UnknownSku`;
+   - a zero, negative or non-integer quantity raises `InvalidQuantity`.
+
+   A rejected request holds nothing, not even for its valid lines, and leaves
+   its idempotency key unused.
+9. An empty `items` or an empty/blank `idempotency_key` raises some
+   `InventoryError` and creates nothing. Which subclass is left to the
+   implementation.
+10. Each reservation gets a unique `reservation_id`. Sequential `RES-0001`,
+    `RES-0002`, … is the suggested format, but callers must not depend on it.
+11. `reserve` never writes to the ledger and never changes on-hand.
+
+### Availability
+
+12. While a hold is `ACTIVE`, `available(sku, warehouse)` is reduced by its
+    held units for that SKU, but never below 0.
+13. A hold affects only its own warehouse: availability of the same SKU in
+    any other warehouse is unchanged.
+14. `place_order` can take only available units (net of active holds). If any
+    line exceeds that, it raises `InsufficientStock` and stays all-or-nothing,
+    as today.
+15. A second `reserve` (with a different key) can hold only what is still
+    available after the existing active holds.
+16. `receive` and `adjust` are never blocked by holds. If a write-off drops
+    on-hand below what is held/allocated, `available` reports 0 and existing
+    reservations stay as they are (same status, same `items`).
+17. On-hand is never changed by reserve, release, expiry or confirm. It moves
+    only through receive, adjust and shipping, as today.
+
+### Expiry (lazy, clock-driven)
+
+18. An `ACTIVE` reservation is `EXPIRED` from `expires_at` onwards, including
+    that exact instant (`clock.now() >= expires_at`). This happens with no
+    sweep or other call in between.
+19. From that instant on, the expired hold's units count as available again in
+    every query and operation (`available`, `place_order`, `reserve`).
+20. `get_reservation` on such a reservation returns it with status `EXPIRED`.
+    Expired reservations remain retrievable indefinitely.
+21. Only `ACTIVE` reservations expire. `CONFIRMED` and `RELEASED` reservations
+    never change status on their own, however much time passes.
+22. A hold can't be extended. Nothing, including an idempotent retry, moves
+    `expires_at`.
+
+### Confirming a hold: `confirm_reservation`
+
+23. Confirming an `ACTIVE` (unexpired) reservation creates an `Order` with:
+    - status `PLACED`;
+    - the reservation's warehouse;
+    - lines equal to exactly the units held. After a partial hold, that is the
+      held units, not what was originally requested.
+    - `placed_at == clock.now()` at confirm time.
+
+    The reservation becomes `CONFIRMED` and its `order_id` equals the new
+    order's `order_id`. The order is returned.
+24. Confirming doesn't change `available` for any SKU: the held units become the
+    order's allocation. On-hand doesn't change until the order ships.
+25. The resulting order is an ordinary order: `ship_order` and `cancel_order`
+    work on it as on any other `PLACED` order.
+26. Confirming an already-`CONFIRMED` reservation returns the same order (same
+    `order_id`) and creates no new order. This holds at any time, including
+    after the original 20 minutes have passed.
+27. Confirming an `EXPIRED` reservation, including one that has only just
+    reached `expires_at`, raises `ReservationExpired` and creates no order.
+28. Confirming a `RELEASED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+29. Whether confirm re-checks physical stock after a write-off is left to the
+    implementation.
+
+### Releasing a hold: `release_reservation`
+
+30. Releasing an `ACTIVE` reservation sets its status to `RELEASED` and
+    returns it. Its units are immediately available again.
+31. Releasing an already-`RELEASED` reservation returns it unchanged, with no
+    error.
+32. Releasing an `EXPIRED` reservation is a no-op: it returns the reservation
+    with status `EXPIRED`, with no error. The status does not change to
+    `RELEASED`.
+33. Releasing a `CONFIRMED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`, and
+    the reservation and its order are unchanged.
+
+### Lookup
+
+34. `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation` raise
+    `ReservationNotFound` for an unknown `reservation_id`.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+35. Two requests are the *same request* when they have the same warehouse and
+    the same requested items, compared as a dict, so the order of lines doesn't
+    matter. The comparison is against what was **requested**, not what ended
+    up held.
+36. Repeating `reserve` with a used key and the same request returns the
+    existing reservation (same `reservation_id`) in whatever state it is now
+    (`ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED` or `EXPIRED`). It creates no new hold,
+    doesn't change availability, and doesn't move `expires_at`.
+37. Reusing a key with a different warehouse or different requested items
+    raises `IdempotencyConflict`, even if the original reservation is no longer
+    active. Nothing is created or changed.
+38. Keys are unique across the whole system, not per warehouse.
+39. A `reserve` call that raised (validation error or `InsufficientStock`)
+    leaves no trace. A later call with the same key is treated as a first
+    call.
+
+### Errors
+
+40. `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`, `InvalidReservationState` and
+    `IdempotencyConflict` are importable from `inventory.errors` and subclass
+    `InventoryError`.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending or renewing a hold.
+- A background sweeper or any explicit "expire now" operation.
+- Changing what `stock_report` shows. Whether held units appear in it is
+  unspecified.
+- Linking an order back to its reservation. Cancelling or shipping a
+  confirmed order doesn't affect the reservation.
+- Re-checking stock at confirm time (allowed but not required).
+- Persistence beyond the existing in-memory repository; concurrency or
+  thread-safety guarantees.
+- Expiring or forgetting idempotency keys: they are kept for the service's
+  lifetime.
+- Partial confirm or partial release of a hold.
